A||||28||TigerFiero||What tires do you have on your Fiero?||||1||
Z||000000||TigerFiero||02-20-2001||08:05 PM||||I just blew a tire this morning and went to buy a new one.  I had bought the car with the tires on it.  They are 187/65R15 Firestone Affinity.  I only needed one so that is all that I bought for now, but tonite I was thinking if I should have just bought an entire new set of something else.  What brand and size do you all use and what do you think is the best for performance, but kind of touring?<P>------------------<BR>TigerFiero<BR>86 Coupe<BR>86 GT<BR>North Florida/South Georgia Fieros||||reg||1
Z||000001||87GT||02-20-2001||08:21 PM||||I have Goodyear Eagle GT II's.  The front size is P205 60 15 and the rear is P215 60 15.  This is the factory spec sizes for my 87GT.  I am pretty happy with my Goodyears.  No complaints and no blowouts. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||02-20-2001||08:25 PM||||I prefer BFGoodrich tires. I have 225/50R15's all the way around.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||LZeitgeist||02-20-2001||09:30 PM||||On my '88 Coupe, I ran BF Goodrich EuroRadial T/A's for as many years as they still made them in 14". Then I switched to BF Goodrich Comp T/A's. <P>For the Formula, it had GoodYear GT+4's on it in the stock sizes. I've always *hated* GoodYears (the ones that handle well don't last worth a damn), so when I needed new tires, I switched back to the Comp T/A's (again, in the stock sizes).<P>I think (since I'll only have to change them two at a time), after the Comp T/A's wear out I'll switch back to the GT+4's, as long as they're not like double the price of the Comp T/A's. I'd like to have the OEM tire for shows and stuff...<P>Time will tell. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||DougV||02-20-2001||10:37 PM||||The Firestone Affinity is a good Passenger tire(The tire size is odd for a fiero though?) I have had very Pleasing results with the Firestone Firehawk SH30(or Bridgestone RE930, same tire). Great handling(Dry and wet) and good in the snow to(Good looking tire helps too!) If you have 15" wheels, stay with the 205/60, 215/60 sizes for best handling. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||RaeQwan||02-20-2001||10:41 PM||||I have round 205/60r14's on mine..<P>the bent wookiee haunts the forum.. hehe<P>------------------<BR>Ray - AIM: WookieeNC<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/ray19nc/" TARGET=_blank>1988 Fiero Coupe</A> - <A HREF="http://www.CarolinaFieros.com" TARGET=_blank>Carolina Fieros</A> - <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/dearabu/" TARGET=_blank>Dear Abu</A> - <A HREF="http://www.webjolt.net" TARGET=_blank>WebJolt Network</A>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Slammed Fiero||02-20-2001||11:09 PM||||Pirelli P Zero C's<BR>225/45/17 front<BR>255/40/17 rear<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>Jonathan M McCreery<BR>86 GT<BR>New Custom White face gauges 17" Tr motorsports Typhoons Ground Control coil overs , Big bars front and rear , Mustang scoop , accel coil , no cat , 4spd , short shift , poly dog bone and bushings momo pedals and shift knob , round headlight conversion , Geni stainless steel oval exhaust tips. Pioneer 6000 CD player , Sony Mobile Amp. Currently doing big brake conversion...<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/jssig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fformula88||02-20-2001||11:21 PM||||I'm running Dunlop D60 A2 (JLB) in 205/60R15 front and 215/60R15 back.  They have been great tires, outperforming the Goodyear Eagle GT+4's while lasting longer.  They stick like glue and I am very happy.<P>I would also highly recommend Perelli P4000 Super Touring.  I run them on my Probe 205/55R15, and feel they give nearly the same great handling ability as the Dunlops with much better treadwear and a better ride.  They are more pricey, but are a very nice tire.  Oh, and they are a thousand times better in the snow than the Dunlops for those who live in the north!!||||reg||1
Z||000008||Haze_Performance||02-20-2001||11:28 PM||||My 1985 Sport Coupe.<P>I use BFG T/A 215/60R14's on stock 14's for winter. Great performance year round.<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/017_15a_3.jpg">  <P>I use Kumho Ecsta Supra 215/40ZR17's on 17"x7.5" MB Mystery wheels for summer. They stick like glue at <B><I>extreme speeds</B></I>.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FronthouseDside2.jpg">  <P><BR>My 1987 SE. (Soon to be White  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">)<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/87fronthouseDsiderear.jpg">  <P>I just picked up a set of 16"x7.5" Eagle Tuners. Im still working out the tire sizes.<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/24207595_2_1.jpg">  <P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.ws6fiero.com" TARGET=_blank><BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ttttttt.gif">  </A>       <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/WS6_Fiero/photo.html" TARGET=_blank><BR>PFF Members Photo Page</A><BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1431213" TARGET=_blank><BR>Photopoint Gallery</A><p>[This message has been edited by Haze_Performance (edited 02-20-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000009||fieroshop||02-21-2001||12:38 AM||||Here in sunny Calif. I am running 215X25/17 in front and 245X45/17 in the rear. They make a big difference in how my GT handles.<BR><P>------------------<BR> <BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fieroshop_sig_1.gif"> ||||reg||6
Z||000010||Neverfinished||02-21-2001||01:39 AM||||I just picked up some BF goodrich comp T/A's<P>225/45/R16's<P>like buttah      baby like buttah<BR>sick ass tires!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000011||onfire||02-21-2001||06:43 AM||||I had Kuhmo 225 50/15's on my Formula I used to own. And BFG Comp TA's 215 60/14's on the 85GT I used to own.<P><BR>Slammed Fiero, How wide are your wheels? Front and rear, and what's the offset? Thanks in advanced.||||reg||1
Z||000012||GT Bastard||02-21-2001||08:02 AM||||Yokohama A509's on my SE.  They also stick like white on rice.  My rear ones started to turn into slicks though.... Not sure how that happens.||||reg||1
Z||000013||stimpy||02-21-2001||09:55 AM||||I have Bridgestone Turanza H Revo in stock 86 GT sizes. Very good all-weather touring tire. Has lots of grip wet or dry, and can actually handle the slushy snow pretty well. (I don't drive the Fiero on frozen snow.)||||reg||1
Z||000014||TheFiero64||02-21-2001||11:05 AM||||I have Michelin pilot's, They are the best tires I've ever had. Here is a description of them. They are a little high priced though. <A HREF="http://www.tirerack.com/tires/michelin/mi_pilotxgth4.jsp" TARGET=_blank>http://www.tirerack.com/tires/michelin/mi_pilotxgth4.jsp</A> <P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>88 Coupe V6<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||3
Z||000015||RRobinson||02-21-2001||11:14 AM||||I have Pirelli P500s on one of my 87 GTs. They are OK but don't like sitting for long periods, and are shaky untill they warm up. Otherwise, I have tried to keep stock sizes and brands. <P>Goodyear Eagle GT+4s are pretty much discontinued at this time. I had a hard time getting P205/60R15 and P215/60R15s two years ago, and I imagine it's impossible now. <P>I have the original old style Eagle GTs on one of my cars and they still work good. This gives me an idea...does anyone know a source for NOS tires like Eagle GTs? I would gladly pay a premium for the 'correct' tires for my Fieros.<BR>Cheers,<P>Ryan<BR>88 GT Red<BR>87 GT Blue<BR>87 GT Black<BR>87 SE V6 Med Red Met<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000016||LZeitgeist||02-21-2001||12:21 PM||||No more GT+4's?? Noooo... *sigh*<P>So in that case, what is a car show judging-acceptable substitute?||||reg||1
Z||000017||KRMFiero||02-21-2001||12:25 PM||||i have 225/60-R14 90S?   on all 4<P>------------------<BR>1984 Pontiac Fiero 2M4 SE<P>  <IMG SRC="http://members.aol.com/~Physcokyle/fiero.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000018||Monkeyman||02-21-2001||07:04 PM||||Pirelli P4000 Touring tires.  They're great in the bad weather (both rain & snow) and are pretty decent in dry weather.  I'll probably keep a set of these for winter driving once I get new summer rims and tires.<P>MM||||reg||1
Z||000019||fierosound||02-21-2001||07:23 PM||||Fiero64...<P>I'm running the Michelin Pilots as well - 205/55/16 front/ 215/55/16 rear.  I've got the all-season ones, and they worked great during the summer heat, and very impressively in show and ice!!||||reg||10
Z||000020||danny boy||02-21-2001||08:00 PM|||| I have 215/60-15 on rear. One is a  continental interceptor, the other is a Kelly Charger. On the left front is a Dunlop D60 A2(205/60-15) and on the right is a Dunlop SP sport 4000(205/65-15).<BR>It handles kinda funny. I used to have BF Goodrich touring T/As on it, but some f**king ex Fiero Factory employee bought them and never paid my for them.||||reg||1
Z||000021||Patrick's Dad||02-21-2001||08:03 PM||||I always wondered about the Dunlops....<P>I wanted BFG Comp T/A VR4's but my wife made me get HR4's. The conversation went something like this:<P>ME: I need new tires for the Fiero.<BR>SHE: How much do they cost?<BR>ME: $500.00 for the set.<BR>SHE: Why do they cost $500.00? (Notice that she did not dismiss me out of hand, or even limit my spending).<BR>ME: Because they're "V" speed rated.<BR>SHE: What does that mean?<BR>ME: The tires essentially maintain their composure up to 149 miles an hour.<BR>SHE: Can your car go 149 miles an hour? (Notice here that she did not ask if I had ever done this.)<BR>ME: No, it's electronically limited to 129.<BR>SHE: Is there a tire that's good to 129?<BR>ME: Yes, the "H" rated tires.<BR>SHE: And how much are those?<BR>ME: $350.00<BR>SHE: Those are the tires that you are getting.<P>I have run 205/60-15's up front (Stock size) and 225/60-15's in back (One size bigger). What a blast swinging the rear end out. And they kick butt on any Goodyear tire I have ever owned (usually came with the cars), including the GT+4. Still, I wonder what the VR4's would have been like, had my wife not outwitted me. OTOH, I think that I'll save up for G-Force's for the rebuild....<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://homestead.deja.com/user.rshuman/files/StarHeroSig.jpg"> ||||reg||2
Z||000022||SteveJ||02-21-2001||08:17 PM||||My House:<P>ME: I need new tires for the Fiero.<BR>SHE: How much do they cost?<BR>ME: $125.00 ( unsaid the word "apiece" )<BR>She: You better get 'em then<P>. . .and I go out and all four( for her safety of course )||||reg||1
Z||000023||2birds||02-22-2001||12:05 AM||||I got an easy 40,000 miles out of a set of Comp T/A HR4's, 205 and 215/60/15.  Twice as good as the Eagle GT or GT+4's I've bought in the past.  I just got a new set of Vipers (directional, H-rated) from Sears.  Four tires and a four wheel alignment (3 hours to get it done right, and I have proof it was done right) for $405 at the Sears in Brunswick, GA.  New Comp's would have cost me another $60.||||reg||1
Z||000024||WampusCat||02-22-2001||11:54 AM||||Another vote for the Michelin XGT-H4 Pilots.||||reg||1
Z||000025||filthyscarecrow||02-23-2001||01:40 AM||||i like pirelli HP's. great in the dry and unbelievable in the wet. <BR>i recommend those or michelins. the skip barber cars run michelin pilots and we torture them. and they take it, and they stick like gum in your hair. <P>------------------<BR>allow me to show you my exhaust pipes...||||reg||1
Z||000026||fierosound||02-24-2001||03:35 AM||||Another thing I forgot to mention about the Michelin Pilots.  I noticed the steering effort greatly reduced compared to the Goodyear GTs and TOYO I ran on it before.  That is why these are the second set of Michelins I've bought... ||||reg||10
Z||000027||87GT4me||02-24-2001||10:28 AM||||Just replace the rear tires on the 87,Viper HX, lasted 15K, 16 yr old daughter is a bit ruff on tires. Bought Yokohoma's in stick size. To early to tell alot about the tire, but ride better that Vipers.||||reg||1
Z||000028||btoth||02-24-2001||01:49 PM||||BF Goodrich Comp T/A VR4's... very nice in snow and rain.  (And it's also cool that they're V rated.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">)||||reg||1
